# Live documentation (must follow on every change)

## Hard rule
If you make ANY change that affects behavior, APIs, protocol handling, UI, config, build, CI, security, performance, or troubleshooting:
- You MUST update the relevant documentation in the same work session BEFORE you finish.
- If no docs changes are needed, you MUST explicitly say: `Docs: no changes required (reason: ...)`.

## Minimal required outputs (every request that edits code)
Before finishing, include a "Docs updated" section:
- Docs updated:
  - <file> — <1–2 bullets of what changed>
OR:
- Docs: no changes required (reason: ...)

## What to update (choose what applies)
- `CHANGELOG.md` for user-visible changes (features, fixes, behavior changes).
- `docs/` developer or user docs that describe the changed area.
- A “status/progress” doc if the repo has one (e.g., `docs/10_dev/status.md`).
- Protocol changes: update the matching `docs/30_protocols/**` page(s).
- Security changes: update `docs/40_quality/security/**` (or the repo’s security doc).
- Troubleshooting/log changes: update the relevant `docs/10_dev/**`, `docs/50_user/**`, and/or protocol doc under `docs/30_protocols/**`.

## Prevent doc sprawl
- Before creating a new doc, search for an existing relevant doc and update it instead.
- Prefer adding a section over creating a new file unless clearly necessary.
- Keep docs single-source-of-truth: don’t duplicate the same info in multiple places.

## Doc drift handling
If you discover something unclear or inconsistent (code vs docs):
- Fix it immediately if possible, otherwise record it in `docs/00_index/KNOWN_INCONSISTENCIES.md` with:
  - file/area, what’s inconsistent, and what follow-up is needed.
